## Founding & History
The Bangor property, an historic 6,000-hectare (or 6,200-hectare) farming estate, has been managed by the Dunbabin family since 1890. The land itself has a history dating back to the early 1800s, having supplied beef to the Port Arthur Settlement, and was also the site of Abel Tasman's 1642 landing. The idea to establish a vineyard began in 2010 when the first vines of Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, and Chardonnay were planted. The Bangor Vineyard Shed, serving as a cellar door and restaurant, officially opened in December 2014. The original shed planned for cellar door use was destroyed in the 2013 bushfires that affected Dunalley and surrounding areas, leading to the construction of a purpose-built structure. Since its opening, the cellar door and vineyard have doubled in size.

## People
Bangor Vineyard Shed is a family-owned and operated business. The current owners are Matt and Vanessa Dunbabin, who are 7th-generation farmers on the Bangor property. Matt Dunbabin was awarded Australian Farmer of the Year in 2015. Both Matt and Vanessa Dunbabin hold PhDs in plant ecology and plant nutrition. They are actively involved in the vineyard and cellar door, often conducting personalized wine tastings and vineyard tours. Head Chef Skye Gamlin leads the kitchen team, focusing on Tasmanian produce.

## Vineyard & Site
Bangor Vineyard Shed has 8 hectares of vines planted. The vineyard was established in 2010. The primary varietals planted are Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, and Chardonnay. The vines are hand-tended to ensure high-quality grapes.

The vineyard is situated at a latitude of 42°53' South, making it one of the most southerly cool-climate vineyard sites globally. It overlooks the seaside township of Dunalley and Blackman Bay. The soil type is described as well-drained clay-loam over ancient dolerite rock. The vineyard has a northerly aspect, which, combined with its coastal location, provides optimal growing conditions and means frost is not typically an issue. The property practices regenerative farming, focusing on soil health, minimal chemical use, and water efficiency.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door is open seven days a week, generally from 10 am to 5 pm, though some sources indicate 11 am to 5 pm. It is closed on Christmas Day and Good Friday. Wine tastings are available daily without a booking, and visitors can join at the bar. Various tasting experiences are offered, including wine and food pairing, wine and chocolate pairing, and vineyard tours. Group sizes from 1 to 140 people can be accommodated for tasting experiences. The cellar door building features Tasmanian timbers and is architecturally designed to offer views over the vineyard and Blackman's Bay.

## Food & Hospitality
Bangor Vineyard Shed features a restaurant that offers an all-day menu showcasing local Tasmanian produce. The kitchen is generally open for meals from 11 am, with coffee, cake, and biscuits available from 10 am, and the kitchen typically closes at 3:30 pm, though some snacks may be available afterward. Bookings are strongly recommended for restaurant dining, especially on weekends and public holidays. The menu is seasonal and includes dishes featuring Bangor lamb, freshly harvested oysters from the nearby bay, craft cheeses from Bream Creek, quail, venison, and local greens. They adhere to a "paddock-to-plate" philosophy. The venue can host private events, weddings, and corporate functions, with exclusive use available for up to 140 guests. Catering options range from morning tea to four-course gala dinners. Special experiences like oyster shucking and "meet-the-producer" activities are also available.

## Awards & Recognition
Bangor Vineyard Shed has received numerous awards for both its wines and its tourism offerings. It has been rated as a five-star winery by Halliday Wine Companion. In 2023, 2022, and 2019, Bangor Vineyard Shed won the Tasmanian Tourism Awards Gold Medal for Tourism Wineries, Distilleries and Breweries. They also received Gold in the 2023 and 2022 Australian Tourism Awards.

Specific wine awards include:
*   Bangor Sparkling (2015) - House of Arras Trophy for Best Vintage Sparkling, 2020 Tasmanian Wine Show.
*   Abel Tasman Pinot Noir (2015) - James Halliday Trophy, 2019 Tasmanian Wine Show.
*   Jimmy's Hill Pinot Gris (2016) - Wursthaus Trophy for Best Pinot Gris, 2017 Tasmanian Wine Show.
*   Jimmy's Hill Pinot Gris (2024) - Best Pinot Gris, Australian Cool Climate Wine Show, 2024.
*   Captain Spotswood Pinot Noir (2018) - Wine Tasmania Trophy for Champion Wine of Show and Pinot Shop Trophy for Best Pinot Noir, 2020 Tasmanian Wine Show.
*   The 2025 Halliday Wine Companion awarded 95 points to their Upton Run 2022 Shiraz, Ladies who Shoot their Lunch 2021 Shiraz, and Stone Dwellers 2021 Mourvèdre. Their Stone Dwellers 2023 Riesling received 94 points, while the Stone Dwellers 2021 Shiraz, The Exception 2022 Merlot, and Ladies who Shoot their Lunch 2022 Chardonnay each received 92 points. The Ginger Prince 2023 Moscato was awarded 90 points.

In 2018, Matt and Vanessa Dunbabin received the Eco Stewardship Award at the Green Carpet Fashion Awards in Milan, recognizing their environmental management at Bangor. Matt Dunbabin was also named Australian Farmer of the Year and Diversification Farmer of the Year in 2015.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Conservation and sustainability are central to the operations at Bangor. The property includes 2,000 hectares (or 2,100 hectares) of private nature conservation reserves and wetlands, and 35 km of coastline. They are involved with the Save the Tassie Devil Program since 2006, caring for a wild population of disease-free devils and protecting other rare species such as Swift Parrots, Wedge-Tailed Eagles, and Sea Eagles. The property is carbon positive, storing 1,500 tonnes more carbon annually than is emitted across all operations. They employ regenerative farming practices that prioritize soil health, minimize chemical use, and optimize water efficiency. Bangor Vineyard Shed holds Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC and EcoStar Accreditation.

## Visiting Practical Info
**Address:** 20 Blackman Bay Road, Dunalley, Tasmania, 7177, Australia.
**Phone:** (03) 6253 5558.
**Website:** http://bangorshed.com.au/.
**Email:** info@bangorshed.com.au.
**Social Media:** Facebook and Instagram.

Bangor Vineyard Shed is located on the Arthur Highway (A9), which is the main tourist route to the Port Arthur Historic Site. It is approximately 45 minutes from Hobart CBD, 35 minutes from Hobart Airport, and 35 minutes from the Port Arthur Historic Site. It is also about a 40-minute drive from Richmond and 60-70 minutes from Orford. The nearest small town is Dunalley. The venue is wheelchair accessible and caters to people with allergies and intolerances.

## Notable / Distinctive
Bangor Vineyard Shed is situated on an historic 6,000-hectare (or 6,200-hectare) property that has been farmed by the Dunbabin family for generations since 1890. The property holds significant historical importance, including being the site of Abel Tasman's 1642 landing and having a history of Aboriginal heritage and convict settlement. Owners Matt and Vanessa Dunbabin, both with PhDs in plant ecology and plant nutrition, are 7th-generation farmers and Matt was named Australian Farmer of the Year in 2015.

The winery is recognized for its commitment to environmental stewardship, with over 2,000 hectares of private nature conservation reserves and being a carbon-positive operation. The cellar door building itself is architecturally designed with Tasmanian timbers, hand-gathered stone, and historic convict bricks, offering views over the vineyard and Blackman Bay. They offer unique experiences such as vineyard tours with Matt Dunbabin, sharing stories of the property's history, and "meet-the-producer" activities with local food artisans. The restaurant emphasizes a "paddock-to-plate" philosophy, utilizing lamb from the Bangor property and other local Tasmanian produce.

